<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Martapura</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Martapura is a town in South Kalimantan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banjarmasin">Banjarmasin</a> on the Martapura river. After a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> attack on Banjarmasin in 1612 the court of the Banjar Sultanate moved upstream to Martapura, which remained a royal seat. It is known as a centre of Islamic learning, associated with the scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-arsyad-al-banjari">Muhammad Arsyad al-Banjari</a>, and for its diamond fields and gem markets.</p></div>
